## Proof

**Setup.** Consider a continuous medium undergoing small deformation. Let the displacement of a particle originally at position with coordinates $(x_1, x_2, x_3)$ be $u_i(x_1,x_2,x_3)$, so that the strained position is $x_i' = x_i + u_i$. The (small) strain tensor is

$$
\varepsilon_{ij} = \tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\frac{\partial u_i}{\partial x_j} + \frac{\partial u_j}{\partial x_i}\right).
$$

**Principal strains and invariants.** The strain tensor is real and symmetric, so it has three real eigenvalues $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2, \varepsilon_3$ — the principal strains — with mutually orthogonal principal directions. The three algebraic invariants of the strain tensor are

$$
I_1 = \varepsilon_1 + \varepsilon_2 + \varepsilon_3 = \operatorname{tr}\varepsilon_{ij},
$$

$$
I_2 = \varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2 + \varepsilon_2\varepsilon_3 + \varepsilon_3\varepsilon_1,
$$

$$
I_3 = \varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2\varepsilon_3 = \det(\varepsilon_{ij}).
$$

These are invariant under rotation of the coordinate axes.

**Volume element aligned with principal axes.** Choose a rectangular parallelepiped volume element whose three edges are aligned with the principal directions of strain, with edge lengths $dl_1, dl_2, dl_3$. The unstrained volume is

$$
dV_0 = dl_1\, dl_2\, dl_3.
$$

